[1] [4] [2] Consider the configuration for the user to only accept WebSocket clients:
```json
authorization {
  users [ 
    { 
      user: foo 
      password: foopwd, 
      permission: {...} 
    } 
    { 
      user: bar 
      password: barpwd, 
      permission: {...}, 
      allowed_connection_types: ["WEBSOCKET"] 
    } 
  ] 
}
```
This configuration restricts the user `bar` to only accept connections via WebSocket clients, while allowing other types of connections for the user `foo`.

The default number of reconnect attempts the Go client makes before it gives up is [2]. To make it retry forever, you set the `MaxReconnect` attribute to a negative value, as described in [2]: ```go
nc, err := nats.Connect("demo.nats.io", nats.MaxReconnects(-1)) if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
```

[1] [4] [2] [3] To make a particular user account usable only by WebSocket clients and not regular NATS connections, the user must be configured with the `allowed_connection_types` option set to `"WEBSOCKET"`. This can be done as follows: ```json
authorization { users [ {user: username password: password, permission: {...}, allowed_connection_types: ["WEBSOCKET"]} ] }
```
